FAILURES
1. ACCESSIBILITY.md point 10; WCAG 4.1.3; SPEC RA1.5. internal/handlers/whatif/handlers_roth_recommendations.go:174 and web/static/js/whatif-roth-conversion.js:60-69. Keyboard Apply from /whatif navigates to same-path fragment #roth-recommendation-applied. After navigation/load and 1 second, #roth-conversion-status is empty, focus is not on #roth-conversion-heading (activeElement.id empty), and the fragment is still present. The success announcement/focus function runs only at script startup; a same-document fragment navigation does not invoke it. Thus the state-changing action does not announce its result or restore the intended focus. This finding rules on accessibility consequences of the traced browser behavior; application persistence remains the tests lane.
Proof: `rtk proxy node /tmp/ra1-actions.cjs` (isolated Chromium, synthetic :8082 only) logged `apply { focus: "", status: "", hash: "#roth-recommendation-applied", schedule: false }`. Earlier independent /tmp/ra1-card.cjs trace reproduced the same result. Changed handler is new; `git diff master -- web/static/js/whatif-roth-conversion.js` adds lines 60-69; no pre-existing attribution applies.
Relevant added code:
+ w.Header().Set("HX-Redirect", "/whatif#roth-recommendation-applied")
+ function announceApplied() {
+   if (window.location.hash !== "#roth-recommendation-applied") return;
+   ... status.textContent = "Roth plan and Social Security claim ages applied. The planner has been refreshed.";
+   document.getElementById("roth-conversion-heading")?.focus();
+ }
+ if (document.readyState === "loading") document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", announceApplied);
+ else announceApplied();

